HISTORICAL / CORRECTED: read MISTAKE-237 and HYP-8905; the three programs are separate.

========================================================================
P1  exact monomial check: P=(x+iy)^d is harmonic with nilpotent Hessian
========================================================================
  P=(x+iy)^2: Delta P = 0 (harmonic)? True ; trace(Hess)=Delta P =0 and det(Hess)=0? True -> NILPOTENT Hessian
  P=(x+iy)^3: Delta P = 0 (harmonic)? True ; trace(Hess)=Delta P =0 and det(Hess)=0? True -> NILPOTENT Hessian
  P=(x+iy)^4: Delta P = 0 (harmonic)? True ; trace(Hess)=Delta P =0 and det(Hess)=0? True -> NILPOTENT Hessian
  P=(x+iy)^5: Delta P = 0 (harmonic)? True ; trace(Hess)=Delta P =0 and det(Hess)=0? True -> NILPOTENT Hessian
  P=(x+iy)^3: Delta^2(P^2) = 0 (VC holds, invertible map)? True
  P=(x+iy)^3: Delta^3(P^3) = 0 (VC holds, invertible map)? True
  P=(x+iy)^2: Delta^4(P^4) = 0 (VC holds, invertible map)? True
  P=(x+iy)^4: Delta^2(P^2) = 0 (VC holds, invertible map)? True
  => these one-sided monomials pass the displayed identities; this is not the full symmetric case.

========================================================================
P2  dimension warning: the general symmetric route is not binary
========================================================================
  The relevant reduction for a general planar Keller map changes the ambient target to four variables.
  Therefore the binary monomial calculation above does not settle that target or JC(2).
  Read the scoped primary-source cards in CORE-PAPERS.md and the correction HYP-8905.

========================================================================
P3  analogy boundary: similar moment syntax is not a transfer
========================================================================
  Laplacian iterates, Gaussian moments, constant terms, and LRC Fourier fibers are differently typed.
  No DvdK bypass or coprime-interval theorem has been mapped to the four-variable VC target.
  A future bridge must state the map, preserved predicate, lost data, and a hostile example.

========================================================================
P4  independent toy: Euclidean descent has Fibonacci worst cases
========================================================================
  max Euclidean steps for pairs < 200: 10 at (144, 89)  ; consecutive Fibonacci below 200: [89, 144, 233]
  the worst-case pair (144, 89) is consecutive Fibonacci? True
  => Lame's theorem: the Euclidean/continued-fraction descent is longest for FIBONACCI (golden CF).
  This finite check illustrates ordinary Euclid only; no JC(2) decreasing invariant is proved here.

========================================================================
SUMMARY -- exact survivors and honest frontier
========================================================================
  SURVIVES: exact identities for P=(x+iy)^d and a finite Euclidean/Fibonacci check.
  DOES NOT SURVIVE: a binary-to-general symmetric reduction, a GMC/LRC/NC2 transfer, or a proved
  Newton/continued-fraction descent for Keller maps.
  LIVE SEPARATE PROGRAMS: four-variable symmetric/VC; planar leading-form/Jelonek geometry;
  Newton-polygon descent with an as-yet-unproved decreasing invariant.
  JC(2) remains open.  See MISTAKE-237 and HYP-8905.
